{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA + GREEN evidence (fix implemented)\n\n### Confirmed mechanism (runtime-verified)\nReproduced the exact common-directory fetch race with a deterministic real-git control in `tests/unit/test_fetch_common_dir_coordination.py::test_uncoordinated_parallel_fetches_can_race_but_coordination_prevents_it`: N linked worktrees sharing ONE Git common dir fetch `+refs/heads/tickets:refs/remotes/origin/tickets` concurrently while origin advances. WITHOUT coordination this surfaces `cannot lock ref 'refs/remotes/origin/tickets': is at <new> but expected <old>` (git's ref compare-and-swap loser); WITH the common-dir lock it produces zero CAS across many rounds. Root cause: remote-tracking refs live in the SHARED common dir, but the sync fetch (`rebar._store.sync.reconverge`) took no cross-process fetch lock at all, and the snapshot fetch (`rebar._snapshot.git_fetch.fetch_origin`) locked only on the snapshot-store path — neither coordinates the other, and the per-worktree advisory git-op lock (`gitutil._store_git_op_lock`, bug 9305) is keyed on the per-worktree git dir and skips the fetch legs.\n\n### Fix (two composed mechanisms, both entry points)\n1. `gitutil.fetch_coordination_lock(repo_root)` — a BLOCKING cross-process flock keyed on the canonical Git common dir (`_resolve_common_git_dir`: follows `commondir` pointers + `realpath`, so linked worktrees and the symlinked tickets clone collapse to one identity). Dedicated fetch lock, NOT the ticket write lock, and the fetch still runs OUTSIDE `.ticket-write.lock` — a blocked remote cannot block a local writer.\n2. Bounded retry of the CAS mismatch (`git_outcome.is_ref_cas_mismatch`, classified distinctly from a held `<name>.lock`) for the residual race with a NON-rebar git peer. Sync stays best-effort (exhausted → skip round, no raise); snapshot fails closed with one actionable exhausted-recovery error.\n\n### Verification (worktree venv)\n- RED-before-fix confirmed: with the retry disabled (`_FETCH_CAS_ATTEMPTS=1`) the sync convergence test fails (`1 >= 2`); with the fix it converges. Snapshot path likewise raised on the first CAS pre-fix, now retries.\n- Deterministic seam-injected retry tests at BOTH entry points + real-git common-dir identity (AC3) + lock mutual-exclusion + parallel coordinated control (zero CAS). 9 new tests pass; stable across 4 repeats (~20s).\n- `make lint` PASS, `make typecheck` PASS (645 files), complexity ratchet new=0 increased=0. Affected suites (sync/snapshot/git_outcome, 140+ tests) green.\n- docs/concurrency.md gains a 'Fetch coordination — the Git common-directory lock' subsection (AC7).\n\n### Attribution\nPer this ticket's Attribution section, the first-introducing commit `c5d79b58b6e829b336684c46cfc482c17c111c7a` has no recoverable ticket; attribution is pre-ticket and no `caused_by` edge is asserted. Layered on current origin/main (tip `cf7c31bec4b6`, after Gerrit 2178/2180), a distinct concern from their broad-fetch scoping."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787565930369437001,"uuid":"1b5db24f-8871-402a-b7c1-eeaf32102a6d"}